/* book Worked example 2: Q: A pressure sensor needs a square diaphragm 1000 µm wide and 20 µm thick on a (100) wafer 525 µm thick, etched from the back in KOH at 1.4 µm/min, with an etch-stop layer that fixes the 20 µm. Find the etch depth, the etch time and the size of the square mask opening on the back face.
   1. Etch depth: d = 525 − 20 = 505 µm.
   2. Time: t = d / r100 = 505 / 1.4 = 360.7 min = 6.01 h.
   3. The (111) sidewalls slope at 54.74° to the surface, so each side of the pit moves out by d / tan 54.74° = 505 × 0.7071 = 357.1 µm between the bottom and the top.
   4. Mask opening = diaphragm width + 2 × run-out = 1000 + 2 × 357.1 = 1714 µm.
   5. Check: the opening is 1.7 times the diaphragm, so a KOH-etched die cannot be much smaller than about 2 mm across. DRIE, with vertical walls, would need only a 1000 µm opening, which is why dense arrays use DRIE.
   Answer: The pit is 505 µm deep and takes about 361 min (6.0 h); the back-side mask opening must be about 1714 µm square. */
